      "id": 1,
      "text": "Millsap began as a relay station on the stagecoach line between Weatherford and Palo Pinto.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millsap%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named after Fuller Millsap, a pioneer who moved to Texas in 1849.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millsap%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1880 the Texas and Pacific Railway reached the area, drawing three nearby communities—including Millsap—into the rail network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/millsap-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A post office opened in Millsap in 1877, marking the town’s first federal presence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millsap%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Millsap sits on Farm to Market Road 113, fifteen miles west of Weatherford in western Parker County.",
